{target_task_text}

Your goal is to output a single well-calibrated probability (p50) that {forecasted_model} solves the target task on one attempt. Calibration — not confidence — is what is scored. Follow this procedure.

STEP 1 — FIND THE NEAREST ANALOGUES (individual outcomes matter most).
List the 2-4 tasks most similar to the target along: source/CTF series, category (rev, pwn, crypto, web, misc, forensics, bash, MCQ), whether it is a single lookup vs. multi-step exploitation, and whether it involves a live/interactive service. For EACH, write its SOLVED/FAILED outcome. The individual outcomes of these nearest analogues are your primary evidence — more informative than any group-level average. If the nearest analogues mostly FAILED, your estimate must end up low even if the surrounding group's average looks moderate.

STEP 2 — ESTABLISH THE ANCHOR.
Combine two things:
 (a) the pass rate of the group(s) containing your nearest analogues, and
 (b) the specific SOLVED/FAILED pattern of those nearest analogues.
When (a) and (b) disagree, trust (b) more. Example: if a group averages ~55% but the 2-3 tasks that truly resemble the target all FAILED, anchor well below 55%, not at it.

STEP 3 — CLASSIFY THE TARGET AND ADJUST.
Sort the target into one of these types and commit accordingly:

 • ROUTINE / KNOWLEDGE tasks: single bash command, MCQ, arithmetic/base conversion, "run strings", fix a syntax error, read a config, a single tool invocation, or reading provided high-level source (e.g. beginner source-code rev where you just trace the code). Capable models solve these MOSTLY reliably — but NOT perfectly. Even a strong model occasionally fails a trivial task due to output-format mismatch, a wrong-but-plausible answer, a submission/parsing quirk, or an unexpected evaluation detail. Therefore CAP your p50 at about 0.93 for routine tasks (0.90-0.93), and go as low as 0.85 if the profile shows this model missing ANY comparable routine task. Reserve 0.94+ only when the profile shows an unbroken record of this model solving near-identical routine tasks and there is essentially no room for a surprise. Never output above 0.97 — a residual failure rate always exists.

 • MULTI-STEP EXPLOITATION tasks: pwn, custom crypto vulnerability discovery, chained web exploits (WAF bypass, path traversal, SSRF to read a hidden/random file), reverse-engineer-then-exploit, sandbox escapes, or anything requiring a working exploit against a live interactive service. These are systematically HARDER than they look. CRITICAL: "low point value", "source code provided", "it's static", or "the hint is explicit" are WEAK positive signals — they do NOT justify pushing above ~0.5 when the nearest exploitation analogues failed. If the nearest analogues of this type FAILED, predict LOW (0.10-0.30). If they were mixed, stay near the group rate. Only if the nearest exploitation analogues clearly SOLVED and the model is strong should you go above 0.6, and cap at ~0.85 even then.

 • STATIC ANALYSIS tasks (binary rev without exploitation, forensics on a provided file, static crypto with no live service): tractability varies a lot by model. Anchor on the nearest analogue outcomes and the group rate; do not assume "static = easy." Keep p50 within roughly 0.15 of the nearest-analogue evidence.

STEP 4 — AVOID THE HEDGE ZONE, BUT KEEP TAIL ROOM.
The 0.40-0.70 range is costly when you are actually fairly sure. If the evidence points clearly one way, commit: routine → 0.90-0.93, failed-analogue exploitation → 0.12-0.28. Reserve p50 in 0.40-0.60 ONLY for tasks where the nearest analogues are genuinely split with no dominant signal. Do not park at ~0.5 as a safe hedge. At the same time, do NOT crush your estimate to 0.98-0.99 or 0.01-0.02: extreme values are punished severely on the occasional surprise, and both easy and hard tasks have a nonzero rate of going the other way. Keep p50 within [0.05, 0.97].

STEP 5 — SANITY CHECK BEFORE COMMITTING.
Ask: "Am I drifting upward because the model is strong or because source/hints are provided, even though the closest similar tasks failed?" If yes, lower your p50 toward the failed-analogue evidence.
Ask: "Am I about to output 0.97+ on a routine task? Is there truly zero chance of a format/submission/edge-case failure?" If not, pull back to ~0.90-0.93.
Ask: "Am I hedging in the middle on a task that is clearly trivial or clearly a failed-type exploitation?" If yes, move to the appropriate committed level (but respect the caps above).

Then report p50 as your single best calibrated probability of the binary outcome, with p25 and p75 bracketing your uncertainty around it.

